# A flat node_modules, and the reason is Windows.
#
# pnpm's default layout puts every package behind
# `node_modules/.pnpm/<name>@<version>_<hash>/node_modules/<name>/`. That
# segment is around ninety characters, and on Windows it makes the Android C++
# build impossible to complete.
#
# **The path is doubled, which is what turns a tight fit into a wall.** CMake
# mirrors a source file's full path underneath the object directory, so
# compiling one file in expo-modules-core asks ninja to create:
#
#   C:\evenglow\node_modules\.pnpm\expo-modules-core@57.0.10_r_8019a…\node_modules\
#     expo-modules-core\android\.cxx\Debug\693l4e5w\x86_64\CMakeFiles\
#     expo-modules-core.dir\C_\evenglow\node_modules\.pnpm\
#     expo-modules-core@57.0.10_r_8019a…\node_modules\expo-modules-core\
#     common\cpp\JSI\MemoryBuffer.cpp.o
#
# — roughly four hundred characters, with the `.pnpm` segment counted twice.
# Real failures, from a real machine:
#
#   ninja: error: mkdir(CMakeFiles/expo-modules-core.dir/C_/evenglow/…):
#     No such file or directory
#   ninja: error: manifest 'build.ninja' still dirty after 100 tries
#   ninja: error: rebuilding 'build.ninja': Stat(…ReactAndroidConfig.cmake):
#     Filename longer than 260 characters
#
# **Neither of the two obvious fixes is enough.** Moving the checkout from
# `C:\Users\<name>\Desktop\evenglow` to `C:\evenglow` saves twenty characters
# against a four-hundred-character path. And `LongPathsEnabled` in the registry
# does nothing here, because the ninja shipped inside the Android SDK's CMake is
# not long-path aware — it uses the 260 limit whatever Windows has been told.
#
# Hoisting removes both copies of the `.pnpm` segment and brings the same path
# to around a hundred and eighty characters, which fits.
#
# ## What it costs, stated plainly
#
# A flat tree means a package can `import` something it never declared —
# pnpm's strictness is the thing being given up, and it is a real guarantee.
# The exchange is that the app can be built on Windows at all, which is the
# platform this ships to first (CLAUDE.md: Android first). CI still runs
# `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` against the same lockfile, so what is
# resolved does not change; only where it is placed does.
node-linker=hoisted
